            <ArticleTitle>Tight junctions, leaky intestines, and pediatric diseases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>386-93</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Tight junctions (TJs) represent the major barrier within the paracellular pathway between intestinal epithelial cells. Disruption of TJs leads to intestinal hyperpermeability (the so-called &quot;leaky gut&quot;) and is implicated in the pathogenesis of several acute and chronic pediatric disease entities that are likely to have their origin during infancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This review provides an overview of evidence for the role of TJ breakdown in diseases such as systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS), inflammatory bowel disease, type 1 diabetes, allergies, asthma, and autism.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A better basic understanding of this structure might lead to prevention or treatment of these diseases using nutritional or other means.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
